window.__labLoaded({"id":"0.3","title":"Population vs Sample","goal":"See that a sample statistic wanders around the fixed population parameter, that bigger samples wander less, and that a biased sample does not.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The lesson's ten shops","text":"Here the population is small enough to see: monthly sales of 10 shops. The population mean (a parameter) is fixed. Each sample of 3 shops gives a different sample mean (a statistic). The population SD divides by N (ddof=0), the sample SD by n - 1 (ddof=1).","code":"import numpy as np\n\nshops = np.array([12, 15, 9, 22, 18, 7, 25, 14, 20, 11])\nmu = shops.mean()\nprint(f'population mean (mu) = {mu:.2f}, sigma = {shops.std(ddof=0):.3f}')\n\nsamples = {'A': shops[[0, 3, 5]], 'B': shops[[6, 4, 1]], 'C': shops[[2, 9, 7]]}\nfor name, s in samples.items():\n    print(f'sample {name}: {s}  x-bar = {s.mean():.2f}  gap = {s.mean() - mu:+.2f}')\nprint(f'sample A sd = {samples[\"A\"].std(ddof=1):.3f}')","out":"population mean (mu) = 15.30, sigma = 5.551\nsample A: [12 22  7]  x-bar = 13.67  gap = -1.63\nsample B: [25 18 15]  x-bar = 19.33  gap = +4.03\nsample C: [ 9 11 14]  x-bar = 11.33  gap = -3.97\nsample A sd = 7.638","figs":[]},{"title":"A bigger, seeded population","text":"Now a population of 10,000 weekly study times in hours, made with a fixed seed so everyone gets the same numbers. We pretend not to know its mean and compute it once: this is the parameter.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\npopulation = rng.normal(loc=14, scale=4, size=10_000).round(1)\n\nmu = population.mean()\nprint('population size N =', len(population))\nprint(f'population mean mu = {mu:.3f}')","out":"population size N = 10000\npopulation mean mu = 13.959","figs":[]},{"title":"Sampling error shrinks as n grows","text":"For each sample size we draw one random sample and show its gap from mu. Then we repeat the draw 300 times to see the typical gap. Bigger samples miss by less. The gap never vanishes completely: that is sampling error, and it is normal.","code":"for n in [10, 50, 250, 1000]:\n    one = rng.choice(population, size=n, replace=False).mean()\n    many = [rng.choice(population, size=n, replace=False).mean() for _ in range(300)]\n    typical = np.mean(np.abs(np.array(many) - mu))\n    print(f'n = {n:4d}: one sample mean = {one:6.2f}, typical gap over 300 samples = {typical:.3f}')","out":"n =   10: one sample mean =  12.40, typical gap over 300 samples = 1.066\nn =   50: one sample mean =  14.58, typical gap over 300 samples = 0.473\nn =  250: one sample mean =  14.04, typical gap over 300 samples = 0.203\nn = 1000: one sample mean =  13.80, typical gap over 300 samples = 0.098","figs":[]},{"title":"A biased sample does not get better","text":"Suppose we only ask the 1,000 heaviest studiers (say, people we meet in the library). Even with a large sample the mean stays far from mu, because the method is tilted. A bigger sample cannot fix bias.","code":"top = np.sort(population)[-1000:]\n\nfor n in [10, 100, 1000]:\n    m = rng.choice(top, size=n, replace=False).mean()\n    print(f'biased sample n = {n:4d}: mean = {m:.2f}, gap from mu = {m - mu:+.2f}')","out":"biased sample n =   10: mean = 21.05, gap from mu = +7.09\nbiased sample n =  100: mean = 20.79, gap from mu = +6.83\nbiased sample n = 1000: mean = 21.05, gap from mu = +7.09","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"shops <- c(12, 15, 9, 22, 18, 7, 25, 14, 20, 11)\nmean(shops)\nsqrt(mean((shops - mean(shops))^2))\nA <- shops[c(1, 4, 6)]\nmean(A)\nsd(A)\nmean(shops[c(7, 5, 2)])\nmean(shops[c(3, 10, 8)])","expect":"mu 15.3; population sigma 5.551; sample A mean 13.67, sd 7.638; sample B mean 19.33; sample C mean 11.33","note":"R, SPSS and Stata have no population SD command, so R uses the formula directly. Random draws would differ from Python's because each program has its own random numbers, so we use the lesson's fixed samples."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / sales inA.\nBEGIN DATA\n12 1 15 0 9 0 22 1 18 0\n7 1 25 0 14 0 20 0 11 0\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=sales.\nTEMPORARY.\nSELECT IF (inA = 1).\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=sales.","note":"The first table shows mu = 15.3 for all 10 shops. The second, for sample A only, shows x-bar = 13.67 and SD 7.638. SPSS always gives the sample SD (n - 1), so for all 10 shops it shows 5.851, not the population 5.551."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 10\ngenerate sales = real(word(\"12 15 9 22 18 7 25 14 20 11\", _n))\ngenerate inA = inlist(_n, 1, 4, 6)\nsummarize sales\nsummarize sales if inA == 1","note":"The first summarize gives mu = 15.3 for all shops, the second gives x-bar = 13.67 and SD 7.638 for sample A. Stata's SD is always the sample version, so for all 10 shops it shows 5.851."},"try":["Change the seed from 42 to 7 and rerun. Which numbers change, and does the pattern (gap shrinking with n) stay the same?","In the biased block, take the top 3,000 values instead of the top 1,000. Predict whether the gap gets smaller or bigger."]});
